    "body": "## Summary\n\nAdds a deterministic test asserting that `restyle()` re-cascades O(dirty subtree), not O(document).\n\n**The regression this catches:** a future change accidentally reverting targeted cascade to full cascade would pass every existing correctness test (the result is byte-identical — that's the ADR-022 cache-equality invariant) but silently regress hover from ~20ms to ~700ms. A timing test would be aimed at this; this test catches it without timing flakiness.\n\n## Measured baseline\n\n- **17 `apply_rule` calls per hover transition** (exactly 1 element × 17 UA rules)\n- Full cascade on the same document: **~1,734 calls** (102 elements × 17 rules)\n- Threshold: **`< 100`** — 5.9× headroom over the baseline\n\nThe `< 100` threshold catches 5× regressions (e.g. accidental double-cascade) while remaining robust to legitimate UA-sheet growth (would need UA rules to exceed ~100 to false-positive, which would warrant its own ADR). Full-cascade reversion (17 → 1,734) fails at any threshold — this one fails fast and unmistakably.\n\n## Production change (named explicitly)\n\nThis is not purely test-only. `src/renderer/css/cascade.hpp/cpp` gains:\n\n```cpp\nstd::size_t apply_rule_call_count();\nvoid reset_apply_rule_call_count();\n```\n\nBacked by a `thread_local` counter incremented once per `apply_rule()` invocation.\n\n**Cost:** one `++` per `rule.selectors.matches()` call. On a full Wikipedia cascade (~1.24M matches), ~0.02% overhead. Negligible, but worth naming since it touches production code.\n\n## Why not a timing test\n\nTiming thresholds flake under:\n\n| Factor | Typical variance |\n|---|---|\n| CI noise (shared runner) | 20-50% |\n| Debug vs Release build | 10× |\n| Thermal state, cache warm/cold | 5-20% |\n| Different CPU generations | 3-5× across dev fleet |\n\nTests that flake get loosened until they never fire, or disabled entirely — providing **negative** value (trained-to-ignore signal).\n\nThis test asserts the underlying algorithmic property instead. It catches the specific regression class that matters for ADR-022's core claim (targeted, not full cascade). It does **not** catch constant-factor overhead regressions — those are handled by ADR measurement discipline: each perf-relevant ADR's Implementation section documents the measured numbers, so a regression shows up in the next ADR's measurements.\n\n## Test\n\n1 case, 5 assertions in `[render-pipeline][algorithmic]`:\n\n- Build a document with 100 sibling `<p>` elements + one `<a>`\n- Hover the `<a>` (null → `<a>`), assert `apply_rule` calls `< 100`\n- Un-hover (`<a>` → null), assert `apply_rule` calls `< 100`\n- Sanity checks that the counter actually incremented\n\nThe `INFO()` diagnostic reports actual values on failure, so if the guard ever fires, the message shows the exact regression magnitude (`hover_calls = 1734` would instantly identify full-cascade reversion).\n\n## Test count\n\n- Before: 259 cases, 1114 assertions\n- After: **260 cases, 1119 assertions** (+1 case, +5 assertions)\n- Zero warnings, zero failures\n\n## Scope\n\nSmall PR (3 files, +81 lines). Two production files (the counter) and one test file (the guard). No behavior change — all 260 tests pass identically before and after.\n\nRefs #147\n",
